{"type":"text","timestamp":1794321368983,"sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","part":{"id":"prt_1c5e34781001VqnlBS5DE58O8p","messageID":"msg_1c5e330c8001R2KaJbxsNAvpdq","sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","type":"text","text":"<think>The deps got caret ranges. The README says dependencies are pinned. Let me pin exact versions to match the repo style (react 19.3.1, vite 8.3.5 are pinned exactly). I'll set exact versions in package.json and reinstall to sync the lock.\n\nLet me edit package.json to use exact versions and add a typecheck script.\n\nAlso add \"typescript\" and \"@types/react\", \"@types/react-dom\"? The project has no typescript dependency listed but the files are .tsx. Vite doesn't typecheck. `tsc` isn't installed. The project uses TypeScript files but has no typescript devDependency — the original minimal project didn't include typescript. Adding typescript + types would let me typecheck. That's a dev dependency addition; it makes the build more robust. But it also adds install time/risk. Hmm.\n\nThe spec says \"frontend with React (TypeScript, built with Vite)\". Having TS types would be good. The minimal project has .tsx files but no tsconfig.json! Let me check.\n\nActually there's no tsconfig.json in the repo. Vite handles .tsx via esbuild without type checking. To keep the build simple and match the existing minimal setup, I'll add a tsconfig.json (good practice, enables editor tooling) and add typescript + @types/react + @types/react-dom as devDependencies so I can run `tsc --noEmit` to verify my code. That improves quality. The risk is npm install size — typescript is a single package, @types/react is small. Fine.\n\nLet me install them and pin exact versions.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794321368961,"end":1794321368975}}}
