{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794321408371,"sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","part":{"id":"prt_1c5e3e14a001J0z2dj32xvFAya","messageID":"msg_1c5e3bcdd001p91jmd5T4ETVFs","sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794321408371,"sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","part":{"id":"prt_1c5e3e14d001qFLg31BzTsmybG","messageID":"msg_1c5e3bcdd001p91jmd5T4ETVFs","sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me set up the config files:\n- `frontend/tsconfig.json`\n- `frontend/vite.config.ts` with tailwindcss plugin\n- `frontend/src/index.css`\n\nAlso add a `typecheck` script: `\"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\"`.\n\ntsconfig.json for a Vite React TS project:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": true,\n    \"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\", \"DOM.Iterable\"],\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"resolveJsonModule\": true,\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"noEmit\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"noUnusedLocals\": true,\n    \"noUnusedParameters\": true,\n    \"noFallthroughCasesInSwitch\": true,\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"]\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"src\", \"vite.config.ts\"]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, `vite.config.ts` uses node types? `defineConfig` from vite doesn't need node types. But `vite/client` types for import.meta.env. Including vite.config.ts in the same tsconfig is fine.\n\nNote: with `noEmit: true` and `allowImportingTsExtensions`, fine.\n\nCareful: `\"types\": [\"vite/client\"]` excludes @types/react auto-inclusion? No — `types` only limits automatic inclusion of `@types/*` packages; @types/react is included via the `import` statements (JSX runtime import) — actually with `types` specified, only those listed are auto-included, but explicit imports still resolve. React types are resolved via `import { useState } from \"react\"` → resolves @types/react ✓. And JSX needs the React types loaded — `jsx: react-jsx` requires `react/jsx-runtime` types which come from @types/react. Since we import from \"react\" in files, the types load. But to be safe, I'll include \"react\" in types? Hmm, `types: [\"vite/client\"]` would prevent auto-inclusion of @types/react global declarations. @types/react is mostly module-based; the global JSX namespace in React 19 types is declared inside the module (React.JSX). With jsx: \"react-jsx\", TS looks up `react/jsx-runtime` module's JSX namespace — needs the module to be resolvable, which it is via node_modules/@types/react/jsx-runtime.d.ts. But module resolution for that doesn't require the global auto-inclusion ✓.\n\nSafest: omit \"types\" entirely so all @types packages are auto-included. Then vite/client types aren't auto-included but I don't need import.meta.env. Actually I might not use import.meta.env at all. Let me omit \"types\" and keep it simple.\n\nHmm, but then @types/node might be needed? Not installed. vite.config.ts doesn't use node APIs. OK.\n\nLet me write the config files.\n\nFor the CSS entry file with Tailwind v4:\n\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n@source \"../index.html\";\n@source \"./**/*.{ts,tsx}\";\n```\n\nWait — in Tailwind v4, `@source` paths are relative to the CSS file. `src/index.css` → `../index.html` = frontend/index.html ✓, `./**/*.{ts,tsx}` = src/**/*.tsx ✓.\n\nAlso add a `@theme` for custom colors? Not needed; use default palette. I'll add a couple of theme tokens for a warm cookbook palette maybe. Keep it simple: use Tailwind's built-in palette (amber/stone).\n\nLet me write the files now.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794321408333,"end":1794321408358}}}
