{"type":"text","timestamp":1794321637277,"sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","part":{"id":"prt_1c5e75f8c001REUlqv7mK2lnxN","messageID":"msg_1c5e74916001X7bTHWR5ope59Y","sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","type":"text","text":"<think>As expected, the browser download is blocked. So no real browser testing.\n\nAlternative: use jsdom to load the built app and check it renders. jsdom is an npm package (available in the registry presumably). I can write a small script that:\n1. Uses jsdom to create a DOM,\n2. Loads the built JS bundle... but the bundle is an ES module with imports; loading it in jsdom requires script type=module support (jsdom doesn't support ES modules).\n\nBetter approach: use vitest + jsdom + @testing-library/react to mount the App component and assert on rendered output. vitest is available on npm? Let me check. This gives a real React render test (jsdom, not a real browser) — good enough to catch runtime errors and verify UI text.\n\nLet me check if vitest and @testing-library/react are installable.\n\nActually, a simpler alternative that avoids many deps: use jsdom + React's `react-dom/client` with esbuild to bundle the app into CJS for the test. But vitest handles TS/JSX natively. Let me check availability of vitest.\n\nLet me try installing vitest + jsdom + @testing-library/react in /workspace/.pwtest (outside the frontend package to keep package-lock.json clean). Vitest needs to resolve the frontend's src files — I can point it at ../frontend/src. That works since vite resolves relative imports.\n\nLet me check package availability first.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794321637260,"end":1794321637274}}}
