{"type":"text","timestamp":1794321763791,"sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","part":{"id":"prt_1c5e94d9b001TCFe0VKHwHAsMY","messageID":"msg_1c5e9216b001oQaO9v5gZBet6x","sessionID":"ses_e3a21d981ffebd45Va6soahg7t","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me write a vitest test that renders the App and exercises the flows against the live backend server (which is running on port 8000). Using real HTTP calls to the local gunicorn server gives an end-to-end test of the frontend logic + API.\n\nI need to configure vitest with jsdom environment, and a setup file for @testing-library/jest-dom.\n\nThe frontend source is at /workspace/frontend/src. I'll create a vitest config in /workspace/.pwtest that includes the frontend src files. The imports in the frontend src use relative paths (./api, ../auth) — all within frontend/src, so vitest will resolve them fine.\n\nThe Tailwind CSS import in main.tsx would break vitest (CSS import) — but I won't import main.tsx; I'll import App.tsx directly and wrap it with BrowserRouter myself.\n\nLet me write the test file at /workspace/.pwtest/app.test.tsx:\n\n```tsx\nimport { render, screen, waitFor } from \"@testing-library/react\";\nimport userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\";\nimport { MemoryRouter } from \"react-router-dom\";\nimport { beforeEach, describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport App from \"../frontend/src/App\";\n\nfunction renderApp(path = \"/\") {\n  return render(\n    <MemoryRouter initialEntries={[path]}>\n      <App />\n    </MemoryRouter>,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nTests:\n1. Browse page shows sample recipes (fetch from the live server at /api — but in jsdom the fetch URL is relative \"/api/recipes\" → jsdom's fetch? Node's fetch (undici) requires an absolute URL. In jsdom, `fetch` is not implemented by default (jsdom doesn't provide fetch); vitest with jsdom environment: global fetch comes from Node (undici) — a relative URL would throw \"Failed to parse URL\". Hmm.\n\nOptions: set the jsdom URL to http://localhost:8000 via vitest config `environmentOptions: { jsdom: { url: 'http://localhost:8000' } }`. But undici's fetch doesn't resolve relative URLs against the document base... Actually, in jsdom environment, `window.fetch` — jsdom doesn't implement fetch, so vitest's jsdom env leaves the Node global fetch in place, which requires absolute URLs.\n\nWorkaround: mock fetch in the test to prefix relative URLs with the base URL:\n\n```tsx\nconst originalFetch = global.fetch;\nbeforeEach(() => {\n  global.fetch = ((input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => {\n    const url = typeof input === \"string\" && input.startsWith(\"/\") ? `http://127.0.0.1:8000${input}` : input;\n    return originalFetch(url, init);\n  }) as typeof fetch;\n});\n```\n\nThat works and tests the real API end-to-end.\n\nLet me write meaningful tests:\n1. Browse page lists recipes with title, category, prep time, author.\n2. Search filters the list (type \"curry\" → shows Thai Green Curry only).\n3. Category dropdown filters.\n4. Paging works (need >9 recipes... the seed has 4; I could add more via the API in the test setup).\n5. Register → sign in → add recipe → appears in the list → open the recipe page → ingredients listed, instructions keep line breaks → edit → delete with confirm.\n6. Sign out shows the sign-in form.\n\nThat's a decent test suite. The delete confirmation uses window.confirm → in jsdom, `window.confirm` is not implemented → jsdom throws \"Not implemented: window.confirm\". I'll stub it with vi.spyOn(window, 'confirm').mockReturnValue(true).\n\nLet me write the tests. Note that the server is running with the data dir /workspace/data (already has dana's lemonade + seeds). The tests will create users; use unique usernames per run (timestamp).\n\nLet me write the test file and the vitest config.\n\nvitest config in /workspace/.pwtest/vitest.config.ts:\n```ts\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vitest/config\";\nimport react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  test: {\n    environment: \"jsdom\",\n    globals: true,\n    setupFiles: [\"./setup.ts\"],\n    include: [\"*.test.tsx\"],\n  },\n});\n```\n@vitejs/plugin-react needs to be installed in .pwtest — it's not. Let me install it (and vite, which vitest bundles... vitest includes vite as a dependency). @vitejs/plugin-react isn't a dependency of vitest. Let me install @vitejs/plugin-react@6.2.1 in .pwtest. Alternatively, esbuild in vitest can handle JSX with the automatic runtime via esbuild options: `esbuild: { jsx: 'automatic' }`. Vite's default for .tsx is the automatic JSX runtime (React 17+) — actually Vite's default esbuild jsx setting is \"automatic\" since Vite 4? Vite's default `esbuild.jsx` is 'automatic' for .jsx/.tsx. Yes, Vite defaults to the automatic JSX runtime. So I might not need the plugin at all. But the react plugin also provides fast refresh (not needed in tests).\n\nLet me try without the plugin first; if the JSX transform fails, install it.\n\nsetup.ts:\n```ts\nimport \"@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest\";\n```\n\nLet me write these files and run.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794321763739,"end":1794321763758}}}
